[38.520488, "o", " so far\r\n  │ That didn't actually reinstall — it thinks pip is already there (because …\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 9 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Now let me verify it works:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 && python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1 && python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 10 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 11 tool call(s) so far\r\npip is working again. Here's what was wrong and what I did:\r\n\r\n**Problem:** The `pip` package files were missing from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/`. Only the metadata directory (`pip-24.3.1.d"]
[38.520546, "o", "ist-info`) remained, so the entry point scripts (`pip3`, `pip`) existed but couldn't find the actual `pip` module — hence `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** I removed the orphaned `.dist-info` directory and re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which properly reinstalled the `pip` package from the bundled wheel.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=11 surface=5dc5182d5448\r\n"]
